Author: Jibin Han <jhan@positron.ai>
Date:   Wed Nov 4 17:52:31 2026 -0800

    cache-mem-perf: run 5c, --asm-passes and the one-core L1 rows (G8)
    
    bandwidth.c: --asm-passes runs all passes of a rep inside one assembly
    call (read and write, one segment, no CME form), so a rep pays one call,
    one loop exit and one return instead of one per pass. The header line
    records asm_passes=0|1. The default form and every other kernel are
    unchanged.
    
    run_followup.sh: phase G8, the one-core read and write rows at 4, 8, 16
    and 24 KiB in both forms under the prefetch counters, into
    bandwidth-l1-passes.csv and bandwidth-l1-asmpasses.csv. 138 commands,
    3.76 s est.
    
    Why: run 3's 16 KiB read row is 58.1 GB/s, 91 % of the two load pipes at
    2 GHz; the missing 9 % is most likely the pass boundary on an in-order
    core, and the four sizes fit it out (follow-up-run-plan.md section 12).
    
    Verified: host build and --quick; on the CRB the --asm-passes read rows
    reach 134.0 GB/s (48 B per cycle) against 129.5 GB/s per-pass at 16 KiB.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

